---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import { SITE_DESCRIPTION, SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title={SITE_TITLE} description={SITE_DESCRIPTION} />
		<style>
			.hero {
				text-align: center;
				padding: 2em 0 1em;
			}
			.hero h1 {
				margin-bottom: 0.25em;
			}
			.hero p.lede {
				font-size: 1.25em;
				color: rgb(var(--gray));
				max-width: 32em;
				margin: 0 auto 2em;
			}
			.section {
				margin-bottom: 3em;
			}
			.section h2 {
				font-size: 1.563em;
				margin-bottom: 0.5em;
			}
			.cta {
				text-align: center;
				margin-top: 3em;
				padding: 2em 0;
				border-top: 1px solid rgb(var(--gray-light));
			}
			.cta a.button {
				display: inline-block;
				background: var(--accent);
				color: #fff;
				padding: 0.75em 1.5em;
				border-radius: 6px;
				text-decoration: none;
				font-weight: 700;
				margin-top: 0.5em;
			}
			.cta a.button:hover {
				background: var(--accent-dark);
			}
		</style>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<section class="hero">
				<h1>{SITE_TITLE}</h1>
				<p class="lede">{SITE_DESCRIPTION}</p>
			</section>

			<section class="section">
				<h2>It's time to go native</h2>
				<p>
					For years, desktop applications have defaulted to Electron. It solved a real problem:
					write once, ship everywhere, and reuse your existing web skills. But we're in a new era
					now. AI coding assistants have changed the calculus — the cost of writing native desktop
					software has never been lower.
				</p>
				<p>
					AI models thrive on explicit, well-established patterns. That's exactly what modern
					native frameworks provide. There's less indirection, fewer hidden abstractions, and a
					shallower stack between your code and the metal.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="section">
				<h2>Rust + GPUI</h2>
				<p>
					The framework at the center of this project is
					<a href="https://www.gpui.rs/">GPUI</a>, built by the team behind Zed.
					It's a high-performance, GPU-accelerated UI framework for Rust. If you've ever written
					React, GPUI feels familiar — elements, state management, and a reactive render loop —
					all running at native speed.
				</p>
				<p>
					Rust gives us memory safety without a garbage collector, fearless concurrency, and a
					type system that catches bugs before they ship. Combined with modern AI models, the
					learning curve is far more approachable than it used to be.
				</p>
				<p>
					The ecosystem is young and growing. Video, rich text, and platform integrations are
					being built out by the community. That's part of the appeal — we're not wrapping a
					browser, we're building directly on the platform.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="section">
				<h2>Why it matters</h2>
				<p>
					Every Electron app bundles its own Chromium runtime. A chat client, a note-taking app,
					a settings panel — each one carries megabytes of browser engine just to draw a window.
					Native applications use a fraction of the memory, launch instantly, and respect the
					resources of the machine they run on.
				</p>
				<p>
					This isn't about ideology. It's about picking the right tool for the work, now that
					the barriers are lower than ever. If AI is going to write most of the implementation
					anyway, there's no reason to keep accepting the overhead of a web stack for desktop
					software.
				</p>
			</section>

			<div class="cta">
				<p>Open-source native desktop applications — built with Rust and GPUI.</p>
				<a class="button" href="https://github.com/going-native">Explore on GitHub</a>
			</div>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>
